    "notes": "Earliest confirmed ancestor. Birth year estimated using a 30-year average generation gap back from Syed Hussain Ali Rizvi (b. 1882–1883) across three generations. The shajra dated the equivalent entry to c. 1650 AD, but the family understands that to have been a rough guess rather than a documented date, so the later estimate is treated as the better working figure.\n\nFamily tradition (unverified): the family holds an oral tradition of descent from Imam Ali al-Rida (8th Imam, 766-818 AD) through an ancestor named Musa al-Mubarqa, who is documented to have lived and died in Qom, Iran, with his descendants (the Radawi Sayyids) centered in Sabzevar before migrating to India, Pakistan and elsewhere. No documented link exists yet between this ancestor and Mir Ali Hussain Rizvi -- the generations bridging that gap are unknown.\n\nA second, related oral tradition: the family recalls a village called Samana with four sub-villages, where three brothers who had migrated from Iran settled across India. Mir Amanullah is remembered as possibly the first of the line to make that journey. A real town named Samana does exist in the Patiala area of Punjab, and is independently documented as linked to a son of the 8th Imam (Sayyid Mash'had Ali, buried there) -- a notable coincidence, though Mir Amanullah himself has not been found in any public record.",
    "notes_ur": "سب سے قدیم مصدقہ جدِ امجد۔ سالِ پیدائش کا تخمینہ سید حسین علی رضوی (پیدائش ۱۸۸۲–۱۸۸۳ء) سے تین پشتیں پیچھے، فی پشت تیس سال کے اوسط کے حساب سے لگایا گیا ہے۔ شجرے میں اسی اندراج کی تاریخ تقریباً ۱۶۵۰ء درج ہے، مگر خاندان کے مطابق وہ محض ایک اندازہ تھا، کوئی دستاویزی تاریخ نہیں؛ اسی لیے بعد والے تخمینے کو بہتر عملی تاریخ سمجھا جاتا ہے۔",
